s were up 11% year on year, led by utility and data-centre customers.\n- Cancellations were below 1%.\n\n**Constraints**\n- **Run-rate gap:** Revenue over the last four quarters was 905 + 960 + 980 + 890 = C$3.735B. Reaching C$4.2B needs about 12% growth over that run-rate, or about C$1.05B a quarter on average.\n- **Halbrook fire:** The Halbrook plant, Voltaris's largest, was offline for nine weeks in Q2 2026 after a fire. Q2 revenue fell to C$890M. Production restarted in August at full capacity (n2.d5).\n- **Catch-up:** Management expects to recover the lost shipments by mid-2027. That requires running above normal capacity, and the plant is already at full capacity.\n- **Backlog conversion:** Backlog is not revenue until it ships. Only about 82% of the guidance would have to come from the existing backlog if all of it converts. The C$5.1B figure may include orders that deliver after 2027, and I couldn't check that.\n- **Industry context:** Voltaris's share of Caldrian transformer shipments fell from 31% to 27% in Q2 2026, and industry shipments fell 4% (n3.d3, n3.d5). Node n3 is covering this.\n\n**Overall:** I would put the odds slightly below even that Voltaris reaches C$4.2B. A small miss of roughly C$3.9–4.2B looks more likely than a large one. The catch-up by mid-2027 is a management expectation, not a disclosed fact. The backlog coverage claim is also a management statement without a delivery schedule.","ttft_ms":2188,"type":"result","duration_ms":25656,"uuid":"7651e844-1acf-4b4b-8e96-f8b73eb33228","ttft_stream_ms":1319,"time_to_request_ms":318,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":13,"system_prompt":26,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":24,"input_attachments":83,"input_hooks":8,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":21,"tool_pool_refresh":1,"init_message":34,"init_frame_send":8,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":9,"message_prep":11,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":2,"request_announcements":21,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":9,"tool_schema_build":11,"